            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Ad budget allocation is one of the core decisions in e-commerce operations. Too little budget means no volume; too much means wasted spend. This article uses a checklist format to break budget allocation into executable steps.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Determine Your Total Budget and Minimum Channel Thresholds</h2>
<p>Before allocating, clarify two things:</p>
<h3>Define Monthly Marketing Budget Cap</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use <strong>10%-30%</strong> of your average monthly revenue from the last 3 months as a reference</li>
<li>New store cold start: increase to <strong>30%-50%</strong>, prioritize finding viable ad solutions</li>
<li>Mature stores: <strong>15%-20%</strong> ratio is relatively healthy</li>
</ul>
<h3>Calculate Minimum Testable Budget per Channel</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Min. Daily Budget/Ad Set</th>
<th>Min. Test Period</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Facebook/Meta</td>
<td>$20-30/set</td>
<td>7 days</td>
<td>Needs sufficient conversion events</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Ads</td>
<td>$10-15/set</td>
<td>14 days</td>
<td>Longer cycle for search ads</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok</td>
<td>$30-50/set</td>
<td>5-7 days</td>
<td>Faster creative burn rate</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Snapchat</td>
<td>$15-20/set</td>
<td>7 days</td>
<td>Smaller audience scale</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: Can your total budget cover the minimum test threshold for at least 2 channels? If not, prioritize testing on one channel first.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Allocate Budget Based on Channel Characteristics</h2>
<p>Not all channels deserve equal investment. Distribute based on your product type and target audience:</p>
<h3>Channel Ratio Reference Formula</h3>
<pre><code>Google Search/Shopping: 40-50% (suitable for products with clear purchase intent)
Meta Ads: 30-40% (suitable for impulse purchases, visually-driven products)
TikTok/Social: 10-20% (suitable for younger audiences, brand awareness)
</code></pre>
<h3>Adjust Based on Product Category</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tools/Functional Products</strong>: Google dominates (strong search intent)</li>
<li><strong>Fashion/Beauty/Trendy Goods</strong>: Meta + TikTok dual-channel approach (visuals and UGC matter)</li>
<li><strong>High-Ticket Items</strong>: Extend conversion path—TikTok awareness → Meta retargeting → purchase (budget weighted toward later stages)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>: What's your product category? Does your current budget ratio match this model?</p>
<h2>Step 3: Differentiate Testing vs. Scaling Phase Budget Rhythm</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't static—it must adjust with campaign stages.</p>
<h3>Testing Phase (First 1-2 Weeks)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Budget: 80% to 2-3 core ad sets, 20% reserved for creative testing</li>
<li>Set total budget cap at <strong>minimum testable budget × test days</strong> for each ad set</li>
<li>Goal: Find creative directions with CTR&gt;1%, CPM&lt;$15, CPC within target range</li>
</ul>
<h3>Scaling Phase (After Testing Passes)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Gradually scale winning ad sets, increase budget by <strong>20%-50%</strong> each time</li>
<li>Maintain <strong>10%-15%</strong> budget for continuous new creative testing during scaling</li>
<li>Shift metrics focus from CTR to <strong>ROAS, CPA, Purchase Frequency</strong></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:<br />
- [ ] Are you currently in testing phase? Is the budget too dispersed?<br />
- [ ] Have you identified viable ad sets?<br />
- [ ] During scaling, is there reserved testing budget?</p>
<h2>Step 4: Establish Dynamic Adjustment Mechanism</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't done once at month start—you need periodic reviews and adjustments.</p>
<h3>Daily Monitoring (Required When Spend &gt;$200/day)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Check if each channel's daily spend deviates from plan by more than 10%</li>
<li>Monitor if ad sets are in Learning Phase; avoid frequent adjustments</li>
<li>Watch frequency—consider prospecting if frequency exceeds 4x per user</li>
</ul>
<h3>Weekly Review Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Does each channel's ROAS meet category benchmark?</li>
<li>[ ] Any ad sets with ROAS below 1.5 for 7 consecutive days? Consider pausing or optimizing</li>
<li>[ ] Any new test creatives showing positive signals?</li>
<li>[ ] Do channel budgets need rebalancing?</li>
</ul>
<h3>Monthly Budget Rebalancing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Eliminate channels or ad formats with consistently low ROAS</li>
<li>Transfer saved budget to top-performing ad sets (top 20%)</li>
<li>Reserve <strong>5%-10%</strong> for new channel or new market testing</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 5: Emergency Budget Handling</h2>
<h3>Insufficient Account Balance or Payment Delays</h3>
<ul>
<li>Alert 3 days in advance for low balance; 1 week ahead during peak seasons</li>
<li>Establish backup payment methods (credit card + PayPal + third-party payment platforms)</li>
<li>Set minimum budget floor for critical campaigns to avoid complete stoppage</li>
</ul>
<h3>Out-of-Control Spending Spikes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Immediately set daily budget caps per ad set</li>
<li>Pause low-value audience targeting</li>
<li>Investigate potential click fraud or bot activity</li>
</ul>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Limited budget for new store, can only run one channel—which one?</strong></p>
<p>A: Prioritize Google Shopping or Facebook/Instagram Feed. These channels have the most direct purchase intent and shortest conversion paths.</p>
<p><strong>Q: One channel has very high ROAS—can I move all budget there?</strong></p>
<p>A: Not recommended. Single-channel dependency is high risk, and traffic eventually saturates. Maintain <strong>70/30 principle</strong>: 70% to main channel, 30% for continuous exploration.</p>
<p><strong>Q: TikTok underperforming—should I cut the budget?</strong></p>
<p>A: First determine if it's a creative or channel issue. TikTok users have longer average purchase decision cycles. If you're measuring with short conversion windows, you may misjudge. Set attribution windows of 14+ days and test more creative directions before deciding.</p>
<h2>Summary: Three Core Principles for Budget Allocation</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Test Before Scaling</strong>: Use minimum testable budget on any channel to validate before scaling</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic Adjustment</strong>: Budget follows data—not a one-time allocation</li>
<li><strong>Maintain Buffer</strong>: Always reserve 10%-20% for testing and emergencies—don't max out</li>
</ol>
<p>Execute this 5-step approach with corresponding checklists, and your budget allocation will be more scientific with more stable ad efficiency.</p>
